WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.115
Why lab leaders’ timelines matter, and how to read them.

2
00:00:03.395 --> 00:00:10.648
The people who run frontier labs make public predictions about when very capable AI will arrive and what it will do.

3
00:00:10.928 --> 00:00:20.315
These claims move capital, shape regulation, and change how companies plan headcount, so as a PM or engineering leader you will be asked about them.

4
00:00:20.595 --> 00:00:27.805
They also come from the people with the best view of unreleased systems and the strongest incentives to sound confident.

5
00:00:28.085 --> 00:00:31.115
Both facts are true at once, and neither settles anything.

6
00:00:31.395 --> 00:00:44.344
This lesson treats each claim the same way, in a claim box: who said it, when, and where; the strongest evidence for; the strongest evidence against or the important nuance; and, where the date has arrived, a score.

7
00:00:44.624 --> 00:00:48.421
The goal is not to decide who is right about 2030.

8
00:00:48.701 --> 00:00:52.904
It is to practice a method you can apply to any claim in five minutes.

9
00:00:53.184 --> 00:01:03.424
One framing from the International AI Safety Report 2026 (February 2026) explains why this matters now rather than later.

10
00:01:03.704 --> 00:01:09.933
It calls it the evidence dilemma: capabilities change faster than evidence about their effects accumulates.

11
00:01:10.213 --> 00:01:17.509
Act too early and you may adopt useless or harmful policies; wait for conclusive evidence and you may be unprepared.

12
00:01:17.789 --> 00:01:26.344
Timeline claims are attempts to resolve that dilemma with forecasts, which is why they deserve scrutiny instead of either belief or dismissal.
